    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-lausanne">Treaty of Lausanne</a> of 24 July 1923 won international recognition for the Turkish state as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>'s successor, and the Republic was proclaimed on 29 October 1923 in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ankara">Ankara</a>, the new capital.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mustafa-kemal-ataturk">Mustafa Kemal</a>'s reforms turned a religion-based, multi-communal monarchy into a secular nation state: a civil code based on the Swiss code in 1926, the alphabet reform, and the vote for women in 1934.</li><li><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> joined <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a> in 1952 after fighting in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korean-war">Korean War</a> and keeps the alliance's second-largest military; coups or memorandums in 1960, 1971, 1980 and 1997 broke its multiparty politics, and a 2017 referendum made it a presidential republic.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Non-Muslims were 19.1% of the population of what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> in 1914 and 2.5% by 1927, after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genocide">genocides</a> of the war years and the population exchange with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>; today they are about 0.2%.</p></aside>
    </section>

