ain the right to vote nationally; the Surname Law gives <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mustafa-kemal-ataturk">Mustafa Kemal</a> the name Atatürk.</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1938</span><span class="rf-text">Atatürk dies and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ismet-inonu">İsmet İnönü</a> becomes the second president; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> stays neutral in almost all of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>.</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1952</span><span class="rf-text">After fighting with the UN forces in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korean-war">Korean War</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turkey">Turkey</a> joins <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a> as a bulwark against Soviet expansion into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>.</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">1980</span><span class="rf-text">The third military coup, after those of 1960 and 1971; the constitution still in force is approved by referendum in 1982.</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">2002</span><span class="rf-text"><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/recep-tayyip-erdogan">Recep Tayyip Erdoğan</a>'s Justice and Development Party (AKP) wins power, beginning more than two decades of AKP government.</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">2016</span><span class="rf-text">On 15 July an attempted coup fails; by 2024 the government counts 13,251 people jailed in connection with it.</span></li><li><span class="rf-year">2017</span><span class="rf-text">A referendum replaces the parliamentary republic with an executive presidency and abolishes the office of prime minister.</span></li></ol>
